From ffb304e2612a9fe6f5d72d5dab1916505a32fbfe Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Koen Kooi <koen@dominion.thruhere.net>
Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 10:42:54 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 50/50] OnkyoAVTCP: fix core bugs and add network standby,
 mute, tone and misc devices

First round of fixes (bugs found reading the plugin against a real
TX-NR575E, cross-checked against independent eISCP implementations
and a live upstream bug report, domoticz/domoticz#5640):

- Zone 3 and Zone 4 selectors used the wrong eISCP command codes
  (SL2/SL3 instead of SL3/SL4). Every other eISCP implementation
  checked (node-eiscp, miracle2k/onkyo-eiscp) agrees on SL3=zone3,
  SL4=zone4.
- The zone/input parsing loop only covered zones 1-3, so Zone 4 never
  received an input list.
- Volume ignored the receiver's actual volmax/volstep, sending/
  reading raw Domoticz 0-100 levels as if they were native device
  units. Now scales both directions using the live volmax parsed from
  the NRI capability XML, with a divide-by-zero guard for zones with
  no volume control at all (this receiver's Zone2).
- Demoted per-packet send/receive traces from LOG_ERROR/LOG_NORM to
  Debug(DEBUG_HARDWARE, ...) -- a receiver that's on and playing
  pushes an NTM update once a second, which was flooding the log.
- sprintf -> snprintf for consistency.

Second round -- the remaining items from the plan's Phase 1/2, plus
new devices from Phase 3 (the ones with an unambiguous, live-probed
wire format; see below for what was deliberately left out and why):

- Fixed the selector "Off" placeholder colliding with real input code
  0x00 (both use the same LevelActions hex-parse path, so any 2-char
  input id could shadow the synthetic Off entry). The placeholder is
  now "100", a value that can never be produced by parsing a real
  2-hex-digit (0x00-0xFF) input code. Existing devices created before
  this fix are migrated in place the next time their zone data is
  processed.
- Guarded the dynamic selector-growth path against exceeding 26
  entries (the most _tGeneralSwitch::level, a uint8_t stepped by 10,
  can represent) -- logs and refuses instead of silently wrapping.
- Added a dedicated per-zone mute switch (On = muted), independent of
  the existing dimmer-on/off mute toggle, which is kept as-is for
  backward compatibility. This also means a zone with volmax=0
  (line-level, no volume control -- e.g. this receiver's Zone2) no
  longer loses its mute control, since it's no longer tied to a
  volume device that might not exist.
- Network standby (NSB) as an On/Off switch -- this is the setting
  that decides whether the receiver stays reachable at all while
  powered off; previously invisible.
- Front-panel dimmer (DIM), sleep timer (SLP) and three simple
  toggles (Music Optimizer/MOT, Late Night/LTN, Cinema Filter-Re-EQ/
  RAS) as selectors/switches.
- Bass and treble (TFR) as setpoint devices (-10..+10), matching the
  existing DucoBoxSerial.cpp precedent for setpoint-style controls.
  TFR carries both values in one packet in both directions, so a
  write to either setpoint is combined with the other's last-known
  value before sending.
- Audio/video format info (IFA/IFV) as text sensors, alongside the
  existing metadata sensors.

Deliberately not implemented, to avoid shipping guessed protocol
behaviour against a real device:
- Listening mode (LMD): controllist advertises Movie/Music/Game as
  "cycle within category" plus Stereo as an absolute code, but the
  exact wire encoding for "start of category" isn't confirmed by any
  source found, live-documented or otherwise.
- Center/subwoofer level (CTL/SWL): live-confirmed at zero ("000")
  but the signed encoding for nonzero values isn't confirmed the way
  TFR's sign+hex-digit format is.
- Net services (NSV), tuner presets (PRS/TUN), transport state (NST):
  research found these consistently low-value/rarely built across
  other eISCP integrations, and this receiver's presets are all
  empty.

Also evaluated whether this plugin's hand-rolled device management
(EnsureSwitchDevice's raw SQL INSERT/SELECT, ReceiveSwitchMsg's own
dedup-and-update logic) still matches current idiom, since the file
predates helpers like SendGeneralSwitch/SendSelectorSwitch that now
do the same job centrally. It's not deprecated -- SendGeneralSwitch
itself is a thin wrapper over the same underlying sDecodeRXMessage
call -- but it is a legacy pattern that a newer plugin (DucoBoxSerial)
in this fork doesn't use. New devices in this commit stay on the
existing table-driven mechanism rather than mixing two device-
management styles in one file; a full migration of the pre-existing
devices to the modern helpers is a separate, larger, and riskier
change than this patch, and is not attempted here.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [WIP]

Signed-off-by: Koen Kooi <koen@dominion.thruhere.net>
